checks={
'pytorch_input_tensors':('Conversion succeeds but the returned feature and target tensors have different observation-axis lengths. Is returning this pair an accepted result?', 'No; the common-batch assertion must reject it before returning an aligned tuple.', ['Yes; torch.from_numpy success establishes alignment.','Yes; permitted target values establish common row length.'], ['Conversion does not check the declared axis lengths.','Allowed values do not repair missing observations.']),
'dataset_minibatch_loader':('A loader uses the declared shuffle policy and batches tuples returned from same-index feature/target retrieval. What relationship must its batches preserve?', 'Each feature remains paired with its corresponding target, even if item order changes.', ['With shuffle=True, features may be paired with unrelated targets.','With shuffle=True, source order must remain unchanged.'], ['Shuffling changes item order, not the relationship within each item.','The declared shuffle policy does not promise source order.']),
'neural_model_definition':('The supplied task requires an unconstrained single linear output per observation. A model preserves batch length but applies ReLU after its final linear layer. Which review is justified?', 'The final activation violates the task contract despite a compatible output shape.', ['The shape alone proves task compatibility.','A final ReLU is required for every single-output task.'], ['Output activation is part of the task contract in addition to shape.','This task explicitly requires an unconstrained final output.']),
'autograd_gradients':('A gradient differs slightly from its chain-rule reference. The declared acceptance rule supplies both relative and absolute tolerances. Which comparison meets that rule?', 'Assert agreement using both declared tolerances and reject a mismatched reference.', ['Require exact equality instead of the supplied tolerances.','Accept every difference because numerical computations may differ.'], ['Exact equality substitutes a different acceptance rule for the declared tolerances.','Tolerance-based agreement is bounded, not permission to accept any mismatch.']),
'cross_entropy':('Inspect the class-index contract: logits have shape (N,C); targets have shape (N,), dtype long and values in [0,C). Which input/loss pairing matches it?', 'Unnormalized logits and the class indices go to CrossEntropyLoss.', ['Apply softmax first because CrossEntropyLoss requires probabilities.','Use class indices outside [0,C) because width does not matter.'], ['The taught loss expects unnormalized logits, not pre-normalized inputs.','The class indices must lie in the declared output-width range.']),
'binary_logits':('Inspect the binary contract: raw logits and same-shaped targets in [0,1]. Which pairing meets the taught loss contract?', 'Pass the raw logits and targets to BCEWithLogitsLoss.', ['Apply sigmoid first, then pass those values to BCEWithLogitsLoss.','Use different output and target shapes because both contain binary-task values.'], ['BCEWithLogitsLoss already combines sigmoid with binary cross-entropy.','The binary-logit contract requires matching shapes.']),
'optimizer_configuration':('A plain-SGD acceptance predicate says the zero-learning-rate fixture changes the parameter. The supplied boundary requires no change. Which verdict is defensible?', 'Reject that predicate: the zero-rate fixture must leave the old value unchanged.', ['Accept it because every optimizer.step must change a parameter.','Accept it because AssertionError cannot result from a false predicate.'], ['The plain-SGD equation gives no change at zero learning rate.','A false assert predicate raises AssertionError in ordinary non-optimized execution.']),
'minibatch_training_loop':('A plain-SGD update record contains parameter_before, parameter_after and current_gradient. Which assertion checks that the step follows this minibatch loss?', 'Compare parameter_after - parameter_before with -learning_rate * current_gradient under the declared tolerance.', ['Compare with +learning_rate * current_gradient instead.','Check only that optimizer.step was called, without comparing the change.'], ['The plain-SGD update subtracts the gradient-scaled learning rate.','A call count alone does not establish the required loss-dependent parameter delta.']),
' training_evaluation_modes'.strip():('For the supplied training dropout fixture, every output happens to be retained and scaled by 1/(1-p). Does that boundary satisfy the masking/scaling rule?', 'Yes; every output satisfies the retained-value rule, and no masked element is required.', ['No; every random training call must mask an element.','No; training dropout must be the unscaled identity.'], ['Random dropout need not mask an element in every call.','The identity is evaluation behavior; training retained values are scaled.']),
'weight_persistence':('A restored checkpoint is readable, but its same-input evaluation prediction fails the supplied tolerance assertion. Which verdict follows?', 'Do not report successful reload; the declared prediction acceptance condition failed.', ['Report success because the checkpoint opened.','Ignore the prediction failure because state_dict comparison is never needed.'], ['Reading the file does not prove preserved predictions.','Both corresponding state entries and fixed-input predictions are required comparisons.']),
'validation_early_stopping':('A reviewer proposes choosing patience by trying alternatives on protected-test observations. Which decision keeps the controller within its taught boundary?', 'Reject the proposal; use the predeclared validation rule and keep protected test out of selection.', ['Accept because patience is not a model weight.','Accept if the chosen checkpoint is later restored.'], ['Tuning settings on protected test leaks information even when no weight is directly fitted.','Restoring a checkpoint does not undo protected-test-driven selection.']),
'training_failure_diagnosis':('The reproduced failing fixture does not meet its declared gradient acceptance predicate. What should the ordinary non-optimized assertion check report?', 'The false acceptance predicate raises AssertionError rather than a successful verdict.', ['Success because the fixture ran without another exception.','Success because an assert cannot distinguish a failed acceptance predicate.'], ['Running is not sufficient; declared loss/gradient/update acceptance must hold.','A false assert predicate is the taught rejection mechanism.']),
'device_placement':('The availability check reports torch.cuda.is_available() is false. Which device selection meets the supported fallback rule?', 'Select the supported CPU device.', ['Select CUDA regardless of the availability result.','Claim CUDA availability based only on the model architecture.'], ['The CUDA choice is conditional on availability.','Architecture does not replace the explicit availability check.']),
' training_batch_sizing'.strip():('Read this authored trial evidence:\n'+json.dumps([{k:r[k] for k in ('batch_size','peak_allocated_tensor_bytes','budget_bytes','completed','throughput_examples_per_second','minimum_throughput')} for r in trials])+'\nWhich criterion selects an eligible setting?', 'Use completed-work throughput among completed settings meeting the measured allocated-peak budget and performance requirement.', ['Use input byte estimates alone, ignoring actual allocated peaks.','Choose the largest batch even if its measured peak exceeds the budget.'], ['Input estimates are not peak training memory.','A larger batch is not eligible when it violates the governed memory bound.']),
' training_memory_diagnosis'.strip():('Diagnostic excerpt:\nsaved_losses.append(loss)\n# loss is connected to the backward graph; no later diagnostic gradients are needed\nWhich review is supported?', 'Saving these graph-connected tensors can retain graph memory; scalar or detached diagnostics avoid the unnecessary connection.', ['empty_cache necessarily frees the graph-connected live tensors.','A saved loss tensor cannot retain a computation graph.'], ['empty_cache cannot free storage occupied by live tensors.','Graph-connected retained diagnostics can keep graph memory live.']),
' training_bottleneck_profiling'.strip():('Timing excerpt: a cold profiled run is compared with a warm unprofiled CUDA run whose CPU clock stops before synchronization. Which review is supported?', 'The comparison confounds warm-up, profiler overhead and incomplete CUDA timing.', ['The measured difference proves an isolated bottleneck improvement.','CUDA synchronization is unnecessary because CPU clocks already include completed work.'], ['The changed measurement conditions prevent an isolated comparison.','CUDA is asynchronous; boundary synchronization is needed to include completed work.']),
' training_hardware_selection'.strip():('Both authorized benchmarks finished, but only one instance has a verified stopped state. Which cleanup/billing verdict is justified?', 'Cleanup is incomplete; stop and verify both, retaining lifecycle durations and any continuing-charge scope.', ['Both compute billing streams ended when the workload finished.','The faster benchmark alone proves the cheaper lifecycle bill.'], ['Compute billing continues while a VM remains running.','Execution speed alone does not establish lifecycle duration times the applicable rate.']),
' task_specific_pretrained_weight_fine_tuning'.strip():('The adapted model and frozen pretrained reference are evaluated on different validation cohorts; the adapted report has lower expected error cost. What conclusion is justified?', 'No matched improvement claim is established; compare both on the same validation cohort with the fixed cost rules.', ['Lower reported cost proves improvement despite different cohorts.','Use protected test to choose the winning adaptation instead.'], ['The taught comparison requires the same validation cohort and cost rules.','Protected test must not be used for model or setting selection.'])}
